// Prehistoric Life, part 4 (27 November 2026): more species and topics.
ADDART({id:"ediacaran",t:"The Ediacaran Biota",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🪸",w:"Ediacaran biota",r:["cambrian","geotime","fossils","trilobites","evo","snowballearth"],
p:[["Before the Cambrian","The Ediacaran biota were strange soft-bodied creatures that lived before the Cambrian Explosion."],["Fronds, discs and quilts","Many looked like leaves, cushions or pancakes on the seafloor."],["Early animals?","Some were among the first large animals on Earth."]],
l:"The Ediacaran biota are a group of mysterious soft-bodied organisms that lived on the seafloor about 575 to 539 million years ago, at the end of the Precambrian, just before the Cambrian Explosion. They are named after the Ediacara Hills of South Australia, where many were found. Most had no shells, legs or mouths that we can see; instead they looked like fronds, discs, tubes and quilted mats. Scientists have long debated what they were. Some, such as Dickinsonia, are now thought to be early animals, while others may belong to groups with no living descendants.",
s:[["Discovery","Fossils of the Ediacaran biota were first found in Namibia in the early 1900s, but their age was not understood. In 1946, the Australian geologist Reg Sprigg found many in the Ediacara Hills and suggested they were ancient jellyfish. In 1957, a schoolboy named Roger Mason found a frond-like fossil, later named Charnia, in Charnwood Forest, England, in rocks known to be Precambrian."],["Strange body plans","Dickinsonia was an oval, ribbed creature up to about 1.4 meters across. Charnia looked like a feather stuck in the seafloor. Spriggina had a head-like end. Many Ediacaran organisms seem to have grown by repeating units in a quilted pattern, a body plan unlike any animal alive today."],["Animals or not?","For decades, scientists argued about whether the Ediacarans were animals, giant single cells, lichens or something else. In 2018, researchers found traces of cholesterol-like molecules preserved in Dickinsonia fossils from Russia, a chemical sign of animals."],["The Ediacaran Period","In 2004, geologists officially named the Ediacaran Period, from about 635 to 538.8 million years ago, the first new period added to the geologic time scale in more than a century. Mistaken Point in Newfoundland, Canada, is one of the best sites for Ediacaran fossils and a UNESCO World Heritage Site."]],
k:[["Lived","About 575–539 million years ago"],["Named after","Ediacara Hills, South Australia"],["Famous fossils","Dickinsonia, Charnia, Spriggina"],["Best sites","Ediacara Hills, Mistaken Point, the White Sea"],["Period named","2004"]],
d:[["c.575 million years ago","The first large Ediacaran organisms appear"],["c.539 million years ago","Most Ediacaran forms disappear as the Cambrian begins"],["1946","Reg Sprigg finds fossils in the Ediacara Hills"],["1957","A schoolboy discovers Charnia in England"],["2004","The Ediacaran Period is officially named"],["2018","Chemical traces suggest Dickinsonia was an animal"]],
s2:[["Why they vanished","Most Ediacaran organisms disappeared around the start of the Cambrian. Scientists think the appearance of burrowing and predatory animals, which disturbed the seafloor mats and ate soft creatures, may have played a part."],["Life before Ediacara","Before the Ediacaran biota, life was mostly microscopic for about three billion years. Stromatolites, layered mounds built by microbes, are among the oldest signs of life."]],
sp:["A schoolboy’s discovery","Roger Mason was 15 when he spotted the Charnia fossil while rock climbing in 1957. His find helped prove that complex life existed before the Cambrian."],
g:[["Ediacaran","The period before the Cambrian, about 635–539 million years ago"],["Biota","All the living things of a place or time"],["Stromatolite","A layered rock built by microbes"]],
f:["Dickinsonia could grow to about 1.4 meters across.","The Ediacaran was the first new geologic period added in over a century.","A 15-year-old found one of the most famous Ediacaran fossils."],
fun:"The fossil Charnia was found in 1957 by 15-year-old Roger Mason while he was rock climbing."});

ADDART({id:"tiktaalik",t:"Tiktaalik",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐟",w:"Tiktaalik",r:["evo","dunkleosteus","fish","frog","geotime"],
p:[["A fish with a neck","Tiktaalik was a fish that had features of land animals."],["Fins like legs","Its strong fins had bones like the arms of four-legged animals."],["A famous transitional fossil","It helps show how animals moved from water to land."]],
l:"Tiktaalik roseae was a large, flat-headed fish that lived about 375 million years ago, during the Late Devonian Period. Its fossils were discovered in 2004 on Ellesmere Island in the Canadian Arctic and described in 2006. Tiktaalik had scales, gills and fins like a fish, but also a flat, crocodile-like head, a movable neck, sturdy ribs and fins with bones similar to the upper arm, forearm and wrist of four-legged animals. It probably lived in shallow streams and could prop itself up on its fins. It is one of the best-known transitional fossils, showing how fish evolved into tetrapods, the group that includes amphibians, reptiles, birds and mammals.",
s:[["A planned discovery","Scientists Neil Shubin, Edward Daeschler and Farish Jenkins searched for rocks of the right age and type, from about 375 million years ago, formed in ancient streams. They chose Ellesmere Island in Nunavut, Canada, and after several summers of searching, found the first Tiktaalik fossils in 2004."],["Part fish, part tetrapod","Tiktaalik had a fish’s scales and fin rays, but no bony plates covering its gills, which allowed it to move its head like a neck. Its front fins had a shoulder, elbow and wrist that could bend, so it could push itself up in shallow water. Its eyes sat on top of its head, like a crocodile’s."],["Breathing","Tiktaalik had gills, but openings called spiracles on top of its head suggest it could also gulp air, perhaps using simple lungs. Breathing air would have helped in warm, shallow water with little oxygen."],["The name","The name Tiktaalik was suggested by Inuit elders of Nunavut and means ‘large freshwater fish’ in Inuktitut. The species name honors a geologist who helped organize Arctic expeditions."]],
k:[["Lived","About 375 million years ago"],["Found","Ellesmere Island, Nunavut, Canada (2004)"],["Described","2006"],["Length","Up to about 2.7 m"],["Name meaning","‘Large freshwater fish’ (Inuktitut)"]],
d:[["c.375 million years ago","Tiktaalik lives in shallow streams"],["c.365 million years ago","Early tetrapods such as Acanthostega live in swamps"],["2004","The first Tiktaalik fossils are found"],["2006","Tiktaalik is described in the journal Nature"],["2014","Studies show its hips and hind fins were also unusually strong"]],
s2:[["Your inner fish","Neil Shubin wrote a popular book, Your Inner Fish, explaining how parts of the human body, such as arm bones and the structure of the ear, can be traced back to fish like Tiktaalik."],["Not the first to walk","Fossil footprints from Poland, about 395 million years old, suggest that some four-limbed animals walked even earlier than Tiktaalik lived. Tiktaalik is best seen as a close cousin of the animals that made the move to land, not a direct ancestor."]],
sp:["Searching in the right place","Tiktaalik’s discovery is a famous example of scientific prediction: the team used what they knew about evolution and rock ages to choose where to dig."],
g:[["Tetrapod","An animal with four limbs, or descended from one"],["Transitional fossil","A fossil with features of an older and a newer group"],["Spiracle","A breathing opening on the head"]],
f:["Tiktaalik could move its head like a neck, unlike most fish.","Its name comes from the Inuktitut language.","Its front fins had bones like an arm and wrist."],
fun:"Tiktaalik’s name was suggested by Inuit elders and means ‘large freshwater fish’ in Inuktitut."});

ADDART({id:"anomalocaris",t:"Anomalocaris",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦐",w:"Anomalocaris",r:["cambrian","trilobites","ediacaran","fossils","shrimp"],
p:[["The strange shrimp","Anomalocaris means ‘unusual shrimp’."],["A Cambrian predator","It was one of the largest animals of its time."],["A puzzle of parts","Its body parts were first mistaken for three different animals."]],
l:"Anomalocaris was a swimming predator that lived in the seas of the Cambrian Period, about 508 million years ago. It belonged to the radiodonts, early relatives of arthropods. Anomalocaris canadensis grew to about 38 centimeters long, large for its time, and had a pair of spiny grasping appendages in front of its head, a round mouth, large stalked eyes and a body lined with flaps that it rippled to swim. Its fossils are best known from the Burgess Shale in British Columbia, Canada. For decades, its parts were mistaken for separate animals.",
s:[["A case of mistaken identity","In 1892, a grasping appendage was described as the body of a shrimp-like animal and named Anomalocaris. Its round mouth was later described as a jellyfish, and part of its body as a sea cucumber. In the 1980s, the paleontologists Harry Whittington and Derek Briggs realized that all these pieces belonged to one animal."],["Hunting","Anomalocaris used its spiny front appendages to grab prey and bring it to its mouth. Scientists once thought it crunched hard trilobites, but studies of its mouthparts and appendages suggest it may have fed on softer, swimming prey."],["Swimming","Its body had overlapping flaps along each side. By rippling these flaps in waves, like a cuttlefish or a ray, Anomalocaris could swim smoothly through the water. Its tail fan probably helped it steer."],["Sharp eyes","Fossil eyes of a related animal from Australia show that radiodonts had compound eyes with thousands of lenses, among the best eyes of any Cambrian animal and well suited to spotting prey."]],
k:[["Lived","About 508 million years ago (Cambrian)"],["Length","Up to about 38 cm"],["Group","Radiodonts (early arthropod relatives)"],["Famous site","Burgess Shale, Canada"],["Name meaning","Unusual shrimp"]],
d:[["c.508 million years ago","Anomalocaris swims in Cambrian seas"],["1892","A front appendage is named Anomalocaris"],["1909","Charles Walcott begins collecting at the Burgess Shale"],["1985","Whittington and Briggs reconstruct the whole animal"],["2011","Fossil radiodont eyes from Australia reveal thousands of lenses"]],
s2:[["Giant relatives","Some radiodonts were even bigger. Aegirocassis, from Morocco, about 480 million years old, grew to about 2 meters long and filtered food from the water like a whale."],["A star of the Cambrian","Because of its size and strange looks, Anomalocaris has become a symbol of the Cambrian Explosion and appears in many museum displays and documentaries."]],
sp:["Three animals in one","The fossil pieces of Anomalocaris were once thought to be a shrimp, a jellyfish and a sea cucumber, until scientists realized they all came from the same animal."],
g:[["Radiodont","A group of early arthropod relatives with grasping front limbs"],["Compound eye","An eye made of many small lenses"],["Appendage","A limb or limb-like body part"]],
f:["Anomalocaris’s parts were once thought to be three different animals.","It swam by rippling flaps along its sides.","Its relatives had eyes with thousands of lenses."],
fun:"The mouth of Anomalocaris was once described as a fossil jellyfish."});

ADDART({id:"meganeura",t:"Meganeura",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🪰",w:"Meganeura",r:["insect","geotime","dimetrodon","fossils","pterosaurs"],
p:[["A giant dragonfly","Meganeura was one of the largest flying insects that ever lived."],["Wings as wide as a hawk’s","Its wingspan reached about 70 centimeters."],["A world of giant bugs","It lived in swampy forests more than 300 million years ago."]],
l:"Meganeura was a giant dragonfly-like insect that lived about 300 million years ago, during the Late Carboniferous Period. With a wingspan of about 70 centimeters, it was one of the largest flying insects known. It was not a true dragonfly but belonged to an extinct group called griffenflies, or Meganisoptera. Meganeura hunted other insects and perhaps small amphibians in the steamy coal-swamp forests of what is now France. Scientists have long debated why insects could grow so large then, and many link it to higher levels of oxygen in the air.",
s:[["Discovery","Meganeura fossils were found in coal mines near Commentry in central France in the 1880s. The French paleontologist Charles Brongniart named it in 1885. Its name means ‘large-nerved’, after the network of veins in its wings."],["The coal forests","During the Carboniferous, much of the land was covered by swampy forests of giant club mosses, horsetails and tree ferns. When these plants died and were buried, they eventually turned into coal. The forests were home to giant millipedes, cockroach relatives and early reptiles."],["Why so big?","Insects breathe through tiny tubes called tracheae, which work less well in large bodies. In the Carboniferous, oxygen may have made up about 30 percent or more of the air, compared with 21 percent today, which may have allowed larger insects. Other scientists think the lack of flying predators before birds and pterosaurs was also important."],["A fierce hunter","Like modern dragonflies, Meganeura probably caught prey in flight using its spiny legs, and had large eyes and strong jaws. A relative, Meganeuropsis from the United States, may have been slightly larger."]],
k:[["Lived","About 300 million years ago (Late Carboniferous)"],["Wingspan","About 70 cm"],["Group","Griffenflies (Meganisoptera)"],["Found","Commentry, France"],["Named","1885, by Charles Brongniart"]],
d:[["c.300 million years ago","Meganeura flies over coal swamps"],["1880","Fossils are found in coal mines at Commentry, France"],["1885","Charles Brongniart names Meganeura"],["1939","The larger relative Meganeuropsis is described from Kansas"]],
s2:[["Other giants","The Carboniferous also had Arthropleura, a millipede relative that grew to about 2.6 meters long, the largest land invertebrate known."],["Today’s biggest","The largest living dragonflies have wingspans of up to about 19 centimeters, less than a third of Meganeura’s."]],
sp:["Insects before birds","Insects were the first animals to fly, more than 100 million years before pterosaurs, birds or bats took to the air."],
g:[["Griffenfly","An extinct group of giant dragonfly-like insects"],["Carboniferous","The period about 359–299 million years ago"],["Trachea","An air tube that insects use to breathe"]],
f:["Meganeura’s wingspan was about 70 centimeters.","Insects were the first animals to fly.","The air in the Carboniferous may have had more oxygen than today."],
fun:"A millipede relative from the same age, Arthropleura, grew longer than a person is tall."});

ADDART({id:"brontosaurus",t:"Brontosaurus",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦕",w:"Brontosaurus",r:["diplodocus","brachiosaurus","paleontology","stegosaurus","allosaurus"],
p:[["The thunder lizard","Brontosaurus means ‘thunder lizard’."],["Gone and back again","Scientists dropped the name in 1903 and brought it back in 2015."],["A long-necked giant","It was a sauropod about 22 meters long."]],
l:"Brontosaurus, meaning ‘thunder lizard’, was a giant long-necked, plant-eating sauropod dinosaur that lived in western North America about 156 to 146 million years ago, during the Late Jurassic Period. It grew to about 22 meters long and weighed around 15 metric tons or more. Brontosaurus is famous not only for its size but for its name’s strange history: for most of the 20th century, scientists considered it the same animal as Apatosaurus, but a large study in 2015 concluded that it was different enough to be its own genus again.",
s:[["The Bone Wars","Othniel Charles Marsh named Apatosaurus in 1877 and Brontosaurus in 1879, during his rivalry with Edward Drinker Cope. Marsh’s mounted Brontosaurus skeleton at Yale became world famous, even though it was given the wrong head, based on a Camarasaurus skull."],["Losing its name","In 1903, the paleontologist Elmer Riggs concluded that Brontosaurus was simply a grown-up Apatosaurus. Because Apatosaurus was named first, scientists used that name, but ‘Brontosaurus’ stayed popular with the public, in books, films and even a 1989 US postage stamp."],["Coming back","In 2015, Emanuel Tschopp, Octávio Mateus and Roger Benson compared hundreds of features in many sauropod skeletons. They found enough differences, such as Brontosaurus’s longer, narrower neck, to treat it as a separate genus again. Not all scientists agree, but many now use the name."],["How it lived","Like other diplodocid sauropods, Brontosaurus had a small head, peg-like teeth, an enormously long neck and a whip-like tail. It probably fed on low and mid-height plants, swinging its neck to reach a wide area without moving its body."]],
k:[["Lived","About 156–146 million years ago"],["Where","Western North America"],["Length","About 22 m"],["Weight","About 15 metric tons or more"],["Named","1879, by Othniel Charles Marsh"]],
d:[["1877","Marsh names Apatosaurus"],["1879","Marsh names Brontosaurus"],["1903","Elmer Riggs merges Brontosaurus into Apatosaurus"],["1989","The US Postal Service issues a ‘Brontosaurus’ stamp, causing debate"],["2015","A study brings Brontosaurus back as its own genus"]],
s2:[["The wrong head","For decades, many museum skeletons of Apatosaurus and Brontosaurus had blunt Camarasaurus-like skulls. In 1979, the correct, long and low head was placed on the Carnegie Museum’s Apatosaurus skeleton."],["How science changes","Brontosaurus shows that scientific names can change as new evidence and methods appear. Its return was celebrated by dinosaur fans around the world."]],
sp:["A famous comeback","When Brontosaurus was revived in 2015, newspapers around the world announced that the beloved dinosaur was ‘back’."],
g:[["Sauropod","A giant long-necked, plant-eating dinosaur"],["Genus","A group of closely related species"],["Diplodocid","A family of long, slender sauropods including Diplodocus"]],
f:["Brontosaurus means ‘thunder lizard’.","The name was dropped in 1903 and revived in 2015.","Early skeletons were given the wrong kind of head."],
fun:"Brontosaurus lost its scientific name in 1903 but got it back 112 years later."});

ADDART({id:"pachycephalosaurus",t:"Pachycephalosaurus",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🪖",w:"Pachycephalosaurus",r:["triceratops","trex","ankylosaurus","dino","paleontology"],
p:[["The thick-headed lizard","Pachycephalosaurus had a dome of solid bone on top of its head."],["A bony crown","Its dome was ringed with knobs and spikes."],["Head-butter?","Scientists debate whether it butted heads with rivals."]],
l:"Pachycephalosaurus, meaning ‘thick-headed lizard’, was a plant-eating dinosaur that lived in western North America at the very end of the Cretaceous Period, about 70 to 66 million years ago, alongside Tyrannosaurus rex and Triceratops. It walked on two legs and grew to about 4.5 meters long. It is famous for the thick dome of bone on top of its skull, up to about 25 centimeters thick, surrounded by bony knobs and short spikes. Pachycephalosaurus is known mostly from skulls, so much of its body is reconstructed from relatives.",
s:[["The dome","The dome was made of dense bone. In young animals it was flatter and more spiky; as they grew, it became taller and rounder. Some scientists think the dome helped individuals recognize their own kind and attract mates."],["Did it butt heads?","Pachycephalosaurus is often shown ramming rivals like bighorn sheep. Some studies found injuries on domes that fit head-butting, and computer models suggest the dome could absorb blows. Others argue the rounded dome would slip, so the animals may have butted each other’s sides instead."],["Growing up","Two other dinosaurs, Stygimoloch and Dracorex, have spiky skulls with smaller domes. In 2009, Jack Horner and Mark Goodwin suggested they were young Pachycephalosaurus at different growth stages, but this is still debated."],["Diet","Pachycephalosaurus had small, leaf-shaped teeth suited to eating plants. Some scientists have suggested it might also have eaten fruit, seeds or even small animals."]],
k:[["Lived","About 70–66 million years ago"],["Where","Western North America"],["Length","About 4.5 m"],["Dome thickness","Up to about 25 cm"],["Named","1943"]],
d:[["1931","A dome is described under another name"],["1943","Pachycephalosaurus is named"],["2009","Scientists suggest Stygimoloch and Dracorex are young Pachycephalosaurus"],["2013","A study finds injuries on domes consistent with head-butting"]],
s2:[["Bone-headed dinosaurs","Pachycephalosaurus was the largest of the pachycephalosaurs, a group of dome-headed dinosaurs found in North America and Asia."],["Fossil domes","Because domes were so solid, they survived as fossils much more often than the rest of the skeleton. Many pachycephalosaurs are known only from their heads."]],
sp:["A natural crash helmet","The dome of Pachycephalosaurus was made of dense bone up to 25 centimeters thick, thicker than a typical brick is long."],
g:[["Pachycephalosaur","A dome-headed, plant-eating dinosaur"],["Dome","The thick, rounded top of the skull"],["Growth stage","A phase in an animal’s development"]],
f:["Pachycephalosaurus means ‘thick-headed lizard’.","Its skull dome was up to about 25 centimeters thick.","It lived alongside T. rex and Triceratops."],
fun:"Most pachycephalosaurs are known only from their skulls, because their thick domes survived when the rest of the skeleton did not."});

ADDART({id:"therizinosaurus",t:"Therizinosaurus",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦥",w:"Therizinosaurus",r:["velociraptor","dinobirds","trex","dino","paleontology"],
p:[["The scythe lizard","Therizinosaurus had giant claws on its hands."],["A plant-eating theropod","Unlike its meat-eating relatives, it probably ate plants."],["Once thought a turtle","Its first fossils were mistaken for a giant turtle-like reptile."]],
l:"Therizinosaurus, meaning ‘scythe lizard’, was a large theropod dinosaur that lived in what is now Mongolia about 70 million years ago, during the Late Cretaceous Period. It is famous for its enormous hand claws: the claw bones alone were about 52 centimeters long, and in life they were covered by even longer horny sheaths. Therizinosaurus grew to about 9 to 10 meters long and may have weighed 5 metric tons or more. Although it belonged to the same broad group as Tyrannosaurus and Velociraptor, it probably ate plants, using its claws to pull down branches.",
s:[["A puzzling discovery","A Soviet–Mongolian expedition found the first remains, including huge claws, in the Gobi Desert in 1948. In 1954, the paleontologist Evgeny Maleev named it Therizinosaurus cheloniformis, meaning ‘scythe lizard shaped like a turtle’, because he thought it was a giant turtle-like reptile that used its claws to harvest seaweed."],["A strange dinosaur","Later finds of arms and legs, and of relatives, showed that Therizinosaurus was a theropod. Therizinosaurs had small heads with leaf-shaped teeth, long necks, pot bellies for digesting plants, and four-toed feet. Feathered relatives such as Beipiaosaurus from China show that the group had feathers."],["What were the claws for?","The claws were too long and narrow for digging and probably not ideal as weapons. Most scientists think Therizinosaurus used them to hook and pull leafy branches toward its mouth, and perhaps for display or defense against predators like Tarbosaurus."],["Size","Estimates based on relatives suggest a length of 9 to 10 meters and a weight of about 5 metric tons, making it the largest known therizinosaur."]],
k:[["Lived","About 70 million years ago"],["Where","Nemegt Formation, Mongolia"],["Length","About 9–10 m"],["Claw bones","About 52 cm"],["Named","1954, by Evgeny Maleev"]],
d:[["1948","A Soviet–Mongolian expedition finds giant claws in the Gobi"],["1954","Evgeny Maleev names Therizinosaurus"],["1970s","Scientists show it was a theropod dinosaur"],["1999","The feathered relative Beipiaosaurus is described from China"]],
s2:[["Neighbors","Therizinosaurus lived in a land of rivers and forests alongside the tyrannosaur Tarbosaurus, the duck-billed Saurolophus and the giant ostrich-like Deinocheirus."],["A popular star","Therizinosaurus has become a favorite in films and games, where its claws make it look fearsome, even though it was probably a peaceful plant-eater."]],
sp:["Longest claws of any land animal","The hand claws of Therizinosaurus are among the longest of any known land animal."],
g:[["Theropod","A group of mostly meat-eating, two-legged dinosaurs"],["Therizinosaur","A group of plant-eating theropods with long claws"],["Ungual","The last bone of a finger or toe, which supports a claw"]],
f:["Therizinosaurus’s claw bones were about 52 centimeters long.","It was first thought to be a giant turtle-like reptile.","It was a plant-eating relative of T. rex."],
fun:"Therizinosaurus was first thought to be a giant turtle that cut seaweed with its claws."});

ADDART({id:"dinoeggs",t:"Dinosaur Eggs and Nests",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🥚",w:"Dinosaur egg",r:["dino","dinobirds","velociraptor","argentinosaurus","paleontology"],
p:[["All dinosaurs laid eggs","Every dinosaur hatched from an egg."],["Caring parents","Fossils show that some dinosaurs guarded and warmed their nests."],["Babies inside","Some fossil eggs still contain the bones of unhatched babies."]],
l:"All dinosaurs reproduced by laying eggs, and fossil eggs, nests and even embryos have been found on every continent. Dinosaur eggs ranged from smaller than a tennis ball to about 60 centimeters long, but even the biggest dinosaurs laid relatively small eggs, because thicker shells would have stopped air from reaching the baby inside. Fossil nests show that some dinosaurs laid eggs in circles or pairs, some buried them in warm soil or plants, and some sat on them like birds. Discoveries of nesting grounds have changed the picture of dinosaurs from cold, uncaring reptiles to animals that often looked after their young.",
s:[["The egg thief that wasn’t","In 1923, an American expedition led by Roy Chapman Andrews found the first widely recognized dinosaur eggs in Mongolia. A skeleton found on top of a nest was named Oviraptor, meaning ‘egg thief’, because scientists thought it was stealing the eggs. In the 1990s, fossils of Oviraptor relatives sitting on nests with their arms spread over the eggs showed that they were actually parents brooding their own eggs."],["Good mother lizard","In 1978, Jack Horner and Bob Makela found a nesting colony in Montana with nests of a duck-billed dinosaur, which they named Maiasaura, ‘good mother lizard’, in 1979. The nests held babies too big to be newborns, suggesting that the young stayed in the nest while parents brought food."],["Titanosaur nurseries","In 1997, scientists found thousands of eggs of long-necked titanosaurs at Auca Mahuevo in Patagonia, Argentina. Some contained embryos with patches of skin. The giant parents probably buried their eggs in warm ground and left them, rather than sitting on them."],["Soft and hard shells","In 2020, a study found that the earliest dinosaurs may have laid soft, leathery eggs, like turtles, and that hard shells evolved later in several groups. This may explain why eggs of some dinosaurs are so rare as fossils."]],
k:[["First widely recognized eggs","Mongolia, 1923"],["Largest eggs","About 60 cm long"],["‘Good mother lizard’","Maiasaura"],["Famous nesting site","Auca Mahuevo, Argentina"],["Brooding dinosaurs","Oviraptorosaurs"]],
d:[["1923","Roy Chapman Andrews’s expedition finds dinosaur eggs in Mongolia"],["1978","Jack Horner finds the Maiasaura nesting colony in Montana"],["1993","An oviraptorid is found sitting on its nest"],["1997","Titanosaur nesting grounds are found in Argentina"],["2020","Scientists report that early dinosaurs laid soft-shelled eggs"]],
s2:[["Baby Yingliang","In 2021, scientists described ‘Baby Yingliang’, an oviraptorosaur embryo from China curled up inside its egg in a tucked position like a bird chick about to hatch."],["Birds still do it","Birds, the living dinosaurs, show many of the same nesting behaviors found in fossils: laying eggs in clutches, sitting on them and caring for chicks."]],
sp:["Ready to hatch","Some dinosaur embryos are so well preserved that scientists can see how they were curled inside the egg, just days before hatching."],
g:[["Embryo","An unborn animal developing inside an egg"],["Brooding","Sitting on eggs to keep them warm"],["Clutch","A group of eggs laid at one time"]],
f:["Oviraptor’s name means ‘egg thief’, but it was really a caring parent.","Even the largest dinosaurs laid eggs no longer than about 60 centimeters.","Some fossil eggs contain embryos curled up ready to hatch."],
fun:"The dinosaur named Oviraptor, ‘egg thief’, turned out to have been sitting on its own eggs."});

ADDART({id:"ammonites",t:"Ammonites",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐚",w:"Ammonoidea",r:["mosasaurus","massext","fossils","octopus","nautilus"],
p:[["Spiral shells","Ammonites were sea animals with coiled, chambered shells."],["Relatives of squid","They were related to octopuses, squid and the nautilus."],["Fossils everywhere","Their beautiful fossils are found all over the world."]],
l:"Ammonites, or ammonoids, were sea animals with coiled, chambered shells that lived from about 400 million years ago until the end of the Cretaceous, 66 million years ago. They were cephalopods, related to octopuses, squid, cuttlefish and the living nautilus. An ammonite lived in the last, largest chamber of its shell, with tentacles reaching out, and used the empty chambers behind it like a submarine’s ballast tanks to control its buoyancy. Ammonites ranged from smaller than a coin to about 2 meters across. Because they evolved quickly and spread widely, their fossils are used to date rocks.",
s:[["How they lived","Ammonites swam by squirting water through a tube, as squid do. The chambers of the shell were separated by walls called septa and connected by a tube, the siphuncle, which let the animal adjust the gas and liquid inside to float higher or sink. They probably ate plankton and small animals, and were eaten by fish, marine reptiles and other cephalopods."],["Telling time with ammonites","Ammonite species appeared and disappeared quickly, and their shells are common and easy to recognize. Geologists use them as index fossils: finding a particular ammonite shows that a rock layer belongs to a particular time, sometimes to within a few hundred thousand years."],["Giants and oddballs","The largest known ammonite, Parapuzosia seppenradensis from Germany, had a shell about 1.8 meters across. Some ammonites, called heteromorphs, had uncoiled shells shaped like hooks, paper clips or tangled knots."],["Extinction","Ammonites survived several mass extinctions but disappeared in the asteroid impact 66 million years ago. Their tiny young drifted as plankton, which may have been hit hard when the oceans changed. Their relatives, the nautiluses, which lay larger eggs, survived."]],
k:[["Lived","About 400–66 million years ago"],["Group","Cephalopods"],["Living relatives","Nautilus, octopus, squid, cuttlefish"],["Largest","About 1.8 m across"],["Used as","Index fossils"]],
d:[["c.400 million years ago","The first ammonoids appear"],["251.9 million years ago","Ammonites nearly die out in the End-Permian extinction"],["66 million years ago","The last ammonites die out"],["1895","A giant ammonite about 1.8 m across is found in Germany"],["2023","Another giant Parapuzosia is found in Germany"]],
s2:[["Snakestones","In medieval England, people thought ammonites were coiled snakes turned to stone. In Whitby, sellers sometimes carved snake heads onto them. The name ‘ammonite’ comes from the Egyptian god Amun, often shown with curled ram’s horns."],["Ammolite","Some ammonite shells from Canada have changed into a shimmering, rainbow-colored gemstone called ammolite."]],
sp:["Living cousin","The chambered nautilus, which lives in the western Pacific including Philippine waters, is the closest living animal to an ammonite in shell design, though the two are only distant relatives."],
g:[["Cephalopod","A group of mollusks including squid and octopuses"],["Index fossil","A fossil used to date rock layers"],["Siphuncle","A tube linking the chambers of a shell"]],
f:["Ammonites are named after the Egyptian god Amun.","The largest ammonite shells were about 1.8 meters across.","Ammonites were used to float like submarines."],
fun:"People in medieval England believed ammonites were snakes that had been turned to stone."});

ADDART({id:"elasmosaurus",t:"Elasmosaurus",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦕",w:"Elasmosaurus",r:["marinereptiles","mosasaurus","paleontology","pteranodon","ammonites","ichthyosaurs"],
p:[["The longest neck","Elasmosaurus had one of the longest necks of any animal."],["A sea reptile","It was a plesiosaur that swam with four flippers."],["A famous mistake","Its first reconstruction put the head on the wrong end."]],
l:"Elasmosaurus was a plesiosaur, a marine reptile, that lived about 80 million years ago, during the Late Cretaceous, in the Western Interior Seaway that then covered central North America. It grew to about 10 meters long, and more than half of that was its neck, which had about 72 vertebrae, more than almost any other animal. Elasmosaurus had a small head with long, sharp teeth for catching fish and squid-like animals, a broad body, four large flippers and a short tail. It is also famous for a mistake: the paleontologist Edward Drinker Cope first put its head on the end of its tail.",
s:[["The head on the tail","The first Elasmosaurus skeleton was found in Kansas in 1867 and sent to Edward Drinker Cope, who named it in 1868. Cope reconstructed it with a short neck and a long tail, putting the head at the wrong end. His rival Othniel Charles Marsh, and the scientist Joseph Leidy, pointed out the error. The embarrassment is often said to have helped start the bitter Bone Wars between Cope and Marsh."],["A long, stiff neck","Studies of the neck bones show that Elasmosaurus could not raise its neck high like a swan, as old pictures showed. Its neck was fairly stiff and held forward, perhaps letting it approach schools of fish from below without being noticed."],["Swimming","Plesiosaurs swam by ‘flying’ through the water with their four flippers, like sea turtles and penguins. Elasmosaurus was probably a slow cruiser rather than a fast chaser."],["Stones in the stomach","Many elasmosaurs have been found with stones, called gastroliths, in their stomachs. They may have helped grind food or acted as ballast to control buoyancy."]],
k:[["Lived","About 80 million years ago"],["Where","Western Interior Seaway (Kansas)"],["Length","About 10 m"],["Neck bones","About 72"],["Named","1868, by Edward Drinker Cope"]],
d:[["c.80 million years ago","Elasmosaurus swims in the Western Interior Seaway"],["1867","The first skeleton is found in Kansas"],["1868","Edward Drinker Cope names Elasmosaurus"],["1870","Joseph Leidy points out that Cope put the head on the tail"],["2000s","Studies show the neck could not be raised like a swan’s"]],
s2:[["Relatives","Elasmosaurus gave its name to the elasmosaurids, a family of very long-necked plesiosaurs found worldwide, including Albertonectes from Canada, which had an even longer neck with 76 vertebrae."],["The Loch Ness legend","Elasmosaurus is often linked to stories of the Loch Ness monster, but plesiosaurs died out 66 million years ago, and Loch Ness formed only after the last ice age."]],
sp:["Head on the wrong end","Cope tried to buy back and destroy copies of the journal that showed his upside-down reconstruction, but the story became one of paleontology’s most famous blunders."],
g:[["Plesiosaur","A sea reptile with four flippers"],["Vertebra","One of the bones of the backbone"],["Gastrolith","A stone swallowed by an animal"]],
f:["Elasmosaurus had about 72 neck bones.","Cope first put its head on the end of its tail.","It could not raise its neck high like a swan."],
fun:"Edward Drinker Cope put Elasmosaurus’s head on the end of its tail and later tried to buy back the journals that showed his mistake."});

ADDART({id:"livyatan",t:"Livyatan",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐋",w:"Livyatan",r:["megalodon","whale","sharks","mosasaurus","iceagemammals","basilosaurus"],
p:[["A whale that hunted whales","Livyatan was a giant predatory relative of the sperm whale."],["Biggest bite","It had the largest biting teeth of any known animal, apart from tusks."],["A rival of megalodon","It shared the seas with the giant shark megalodon."]],
l:"Livyatan melvillei was a giant predatory whale that lived about 9.9 to 8.9 million years ago, during the Miocene Epoch, in the Pacific Ocean off what is now Peru. A relative of today’s sperm whale, it is known from a huge skull with jaws and teeth found in 2008 in the desert of the Pisco Formation and described in 2010. Its largest teeth were about 36 centimeters long, the biggest biting teeth known from any animal, excluding tusks. Estimates of its length range from about 12 to 17 meters. Livyatan probably hunted other whales, as did the giant shark megalodon, which lived at the same time.",
s:[["Discovery","The skull was found in November 2008 by Klaas Post of the Natural History Museum Rotterdam, on the last day of a field trip in the Peruvian desert. The fossil was described by a team led by Olivier Lambert in the journal Nature in 2010."],["A different kind of sperm whale","Modern sperm whales have teeth only in their lower jaws and feed mostly on squid by suction. Livyatan had large teeth in both upper and lower jaws and a powerful bite, suggesting it attacked large prey, such as baleen whales, seals and dolphins."],["The name","The team first named it Leviathan melvillei, after the biblical sea monster and Herman Melville, author of Moby-Dick. Because the name Leviathan had already been used for another animal, they changed it in 2010 to Livyatan, from the Hebrew spelling."],["Sharing the seas","Livyatan and megalodon lived in the same waters at the same time and probably competed for prey. Teeth of Livyatan-like whales have also been found in Chile, Australia, South Africa and the United States."]],
k:[["Lived","About 9.9–8.9 million years ago"],["Where","Pisco Formation, Peru"],["Length","About 12–17 m (estimates vary)"],["Largest teeth","About 36 cm"],["Named after","Leviathan and Herman Melville"]],
d:[["c.9.9 million years ago","Livyatan hunts in the Pacific off Peru"],["2008","The skull is found in the Peruvian desert"],["2010","Livyatan is described and renamed"],["2025","A new study suggests a smaller length of about 12–13 m"]],
s2:[["The Pisco Formation","The Pisco desert in Peru was once a shallow sea. Its rocks are packed with fossil whales, seals, penguins, sharks and even aquatic sloths, making it one of the richest fossil sites for ocean life."],["Today’s sperm whales","Sperm whales are the largest toothed predators alive today, diving more than 1,000 meters to hunt squid. Their ancestors included giants like Livyatan."]],
sp:["Biggest bite in history","Livyatan’s 36-centimeter teeth are longer than the largest teeth of T. rex or megalodon."],
g:[["Sperm whale","The largest living toothed whale"],["Miocene","The epoch about 23–5.3 million years ago"],["Baleen whale","A whale that filters food with baleen plates"]],
f:["Livyatan had the largest biting teeth of any known animal, excluding tusks.","It was named partly after the author of Moby-Dick.","It lived at the same time as megalodon."],
fun:"Livyatan’s name honors Herman Melville, the author of the whale novel Moby-Dick."});

ADDART({id:"woollyrhino",t:"Woolly Rhinoceros",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦏",w:"Woolly rhinoceros",r:["mammoth","iceagemammals","caveart","iceage","smilodon"],
p:[["A rhino for the cold","The woolly rhinoceros was covered in thick fur to survive the Ice Age."],["A giant horn","Its front horn could be more than a meter long."],["Painted by early people","Stone Age artists drew it on cave walls."]],
l:"The woolly rhinoceros was a large, shaggy rhinoceros that lived across northern Europe and Asia during the Ice Age, from more than 450,000 years ago until about 14,000 years ago. It was about 3.2 to 3.6 meters long and up to about 1.6 meters tall at the shoulder, and it weighed around 1.5 to 2 metric tons. It had thick fur, small ears, a short tail and two horns, the front one often more than a meter long. It grazed on grasses and other plants of the cold mammoth steppe. Frozen carcasses found in Siberia and paintings in caves such as Chauvet in France show what it looked like.",
s:[["Built for the cold","Woolly rhinos had long, thick fur, a layer of fat under the skin, and small ears and tail to reduce heat loss. Their wide front horn may have been used to sweep snow away from plants, and its sides show wear from this."],["Frozen in time","In 2014, hunters in Yakutia, Siberia, found a mummified baby woolly rhino, nicknamed Sasha, with its fur, horns and soft tissues preserved. Earlier, in 1929, scientists dug up remarkably preserved rhinos from an oil-soaked pit at Starunia, in what is now Ukraine."],["In cave art","Ice Age artists painted and carved woolly rhinos more than 31,000 years ago, at Chauvet Cave in France and elsewhere. Some pictures show the hump on the shoulders and the long horns, details later confirmed by frozen specimens."],["Why it disappeared","The woolly rhino’s range shrank toward northeastern Siberia after about 17,000 years ago. Genetic studies suggest that its numbers stayed stable until shortly before it vanished, pointing to climate warming at the end of the Ice Age, which changed its grassland habitat, as the main cause, possibly with some help from human hunting."]],
k:[["Lived","More than 450,000 to about 14,000 years ago"],["Where","Northern Europe and Asia"],["Length","About 3.2–3.6 m"],["Weight","About 1.5–2 metric tons"],["Front horn","Often over 1 m"]],
d:[["c.450,000 years ago","Woolly rhinos live in Europe"],["c.31,000 years ago","Woolly rhinos are painted in Chauvet Cave"],["c.14,000 years ago","The last woolly rhinos die out in Siberia"],["1929","Preserved rhinos are excavated at Starunia"],["2014","The frozen baby rhino Sasha is found in Siberia"]],
s2:[["Living relatives","The woolly rhino’s closest living relative is the Sumatran rhinoceros, the smallest and hairiest living rhino, which is critically endangered."],["Unicorn legends","Some scientists have suggested that horns of woolly rhinos and the related Elasmotherium, found in the ground, may have inspired legends of unicorns."]],
sp:["Snowplow horn","Wear marks on woolly rhino horns suggest they swept them side to side to clear snow off the plants they ate."],
g:[["Mammoth steppe","The cold grassland of the Ice Age"],["Mummified","Preserved with skin and soft tissues"],["Megafauna","Large animals"]],
f:["The woolly rhino’s front horn could be more than a meter long.","A frozen baby rhino named Sasha was found in 2014.","Its closest living relative is the Sumatran rhino."],
fun:"Woolly rhinos may have used their long horns like snowplows to clear snow off grass."});

ADDART({id:"groundsloths",t:"Giant Ground Sloths",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦥",w:"Megatherium",r:["iceagemammals","smilodon","mammoth","darwin","sloth","glyptodon"],
p:[["Sloths the size of elephants","Giant ground sloths were huge relatives of today’s tree sloths."],["Megatherium","The largest, Megatherium, was about 6 meters long."],["Gone by the end of the Ice Age","They vanished around 12,000 years ago."]],
l:"Giant ground sloths were large, plant-eating mammals that lived in the Americas, relatives of the small tree sloths alive today. Instead of hanging in trees, they walked on the ground, some on all fours and some able to rear up on their back legs and thick tails to reach leaves. The biggest, Megatherium americanum of South America, was about 6 meters long and weighed around 4 metric tons, as much as an elephant. Ground sloths had huge claws, which they used to pull down branches and defend themselves. Most ground sloths died out around 12,000 years ago, near the end of the last Ice Age.",
s:[["Megatherium","The first Megatherium skeleton was found in 1787 near the Luján River in what is now Argentina and shipped to Madrid, Spain. In 1796, the French scientist Georges Cuvier studied drawings of it and named it Megatherium, meaning ‘great beast’. He realized it was a giant relative of sloths and used it as evidence that species could become extinct."],["Darwin’s sloths","During the voyage of the Beagle in the 1830s, Charles Darwin collected fossil bones of giant ground sloths and other extinct mammals in South America. Their resemblance to living sloths and armadillos helped him think about how species change over time."],["How they lived","Ground sloths had broad hips and thick tails that supported them when they stood up. Their claws were so large that some walked on the sides of their feet or on their knuckles. Fossilized dung found in caves shows that some ate desert plants, grasses and leaves."],["Many kinds","Ground sloths came in many sizes, from about the size of a dog to Megatherium. Some, like Eremotherium, lived as far north as the United States, and others reached the Caribbean islands, where some survived until about 4,500 years ago. One group, Thalassocnus, even swam in the sea off Peru."]],
k:[["Largest","Megatherium americanum, about 6 m long"],["Weight","About 4 metric tons"],["Where","The Americas"],["Living relatives","Tree sloths, armadillos, anteaters"],["Extinct","About 12,000 years ago (mainland)"]],
d:[["c.3.6 million years ago","The earliest Megatherium fossils"],["1787","A Megatherium skeleton is found in Argentina"],["1796","Georges Cuvier names Megatherium"],["1832","Charles Darwin collects ground sloth fossils in Argentina"],["c.12,000 years ago","Megatherium and most mainland ground sloths die out"]],
s2:[["Sloths and people","Ground sloths were still alive when people arrived in the Americas. Footprints in New Mexico show people following a giant ground sloth, and the sloth rearing up to face them."],["Island survivors","Ground sloths on Caribbean islands such as Cuba and Hispaniola survived thousands of years longer than those on the mainland, disappearing only after people reached the islands."]],
sp:["A sloth that could stand","Megatherium could rise on its back legs and lean on its tail like a tripod, raising its head about 4 meters to reach treetops."],
g:[["Ground sloth","An extinct sloth that lived on the ground"],["Megatherium","The largest ground sloth, meaning ‘great beast’"],["Xenarthra","The group including sloths, armadillos and anteaters"]],
f:["Megatherium was as heavy as an elephant.","Darwin collected ground sloth fossils on the Beagle voyage.","Some ground sloths swam in the sea."],
fun:"Fossil footprints in New Mexico show ancient people following a giant ground sloth."});

ADDART({id:"direwolf",t:"The Dire Wolf",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐺",w:"Dire wolf",r:["smilodon","iceagemammals","wolf","dog","genetics"],
p:[["An Ice Age hunter","The dire wolf was a large dog-like predator of the Americas."],["Not just a big gray wolf","DNA shows it belonged to a separate, ancient lineage."],["Made famous by fiction","Fantasy stories and a 2025 gene-editing project brought it into the news."]],
l:"The dire wolf, Aenocyon dirus, was a large, dog-like predator that lived in North and South America from about 250,000 to 13,000 years ago, during the Late Pleistocene. It was a little larger and heavier than a gray wolf, weighing about 68 kilograms, with a broad head and strong teeth for crushing bone. Dire wolves hunted large animals such as horses, bison and ground sloths. Thousands of their skeletons have been found in the La Brea Tar Pits in Los Angeles. A 2021 DNA study showed that dire wolves were not close relatives of gray wolves but the last members of a lineage that split off millions of years ago.",
s:[["La Brea","More than 4,000 dire wolves have been dug from the La Brea Tar Pits, more than any other mammal there. They were probably drawn to animals trapped in the sticky asphalt and became stuck themselves. The large numbers suggest that dire wolves lived and hunted in packs."],["A separate lineage","Dire wolves looked much like large gray wolves, so they were long classified in the same genus, Canis. In 2021, scientists who studied ancient DNA found that dire wolves split from the ancestors of wolves, coyotes and jackals about 5.7 million years ago. They were placed in their own genus, Aenocyon."],["Extinction","Dire wolves disappeared around 13,000 years ago, along with many of the large animals they hunted. Gray wolves, which could eat smaller prey and adapt to new habitats, survived."],["The 2025 ‘dire wolves’","In April 2025, the company Colossal Biosciences announced three wolf pups, named Romulus, Remus and Khaleesi, made by editing 14 genes of gray wolf cells to give them dire wolf traits such as a larger size and a light coat. Many scientists, and the IUCN’s canid experts, said the animals are modified gray wolves, not true dire wolves."]],
k:[["Scientific name","Aenocyon dirus"],["Lived","About 250,000–13,000 years ago"],["Weight","About 68 kg"],["Most found at","La Brea Tar Pits (over 4,000)"],["Split from wolves","About 5.7 million years ago"]],
d:[["1858","Joseph Leidy names the dire wolf from a fossil found in Indiana"],["1913","Excavations at La Brea begin to uncover thousands of dire wolves"],["2011","Game of Thrones makes dire wolves famous on television"],["2021","DNA shows dire wolves were a separate lineage"],["2025","A company announces gene-edited wolves with dire wolf traits"]],
s2:[["In fantasy","Dire wolves appear in many fantasy stories and games. The television series Game of Thrones, based on George R. R. Martin’s novels, made the animal widely known, though its giant fictional wolves are far bigger than the real ones."],["De-extinction debate","The 2025 announcement sparked debate about whether extinct species can truly be brought back, and whether money would be better spent protecting endangered animals alive today."]],
sp:["Pack hunters","Healed injuries on many La Brea dire wolf skeletons suggest that injured wolves survived, perhaps because pack members shared food with them."],
g:[["Lineage","A line of descent from a common ancestor"],["Ancient DNA","Genetic material recovered from old remains"],["De-extinction","Efforts to revive extinct species or their traits"]],
f:["More than 4,000 dire wolves have been found at La Brea.","Dire wolves split from the ancestors of gray wolves about 5.7 million years ago.","Scientists say the 2025 ‘dire wolves’ are modified gray wolves."],
fun:"More dire wolves have been found in the La Brea Tar Pits than any other mammal."});
